Thermal State Engine (TS-Engine)

A Thermophysiology-Constrained Hybrid Optimal-Control Framework for Exergy-Aware Thermal Regulation

Ouadi Maakoul 

AI : chatGpt, Deepseek, Meta, Kimi 

Abstract

This document presents the complete theoretical specification of the Thermal State Engine (TS-Engine), a thermophysiology-constrained hybrid optimal-control framework for exergy-aware thermal regulation of occupied environments. The central thesis is that thermal regulation is not a setpoint-tracking problem but a hybrid optimal control problem over a thermal equivalence manifold, where the decision variable is the dynamic selection and coordination of heat-transfer mechanisms driven by an explicit human thermophysiological state.

The framework rests on three falsifiable hypotheses. H₁ (Manifold non-triviality) states that under non-degeneracy the thermal equivalence manifold ℰ(𝐱_H*) has positive dimension M + P − rank(DF). H₂ (Non-constant exergy cost) states that ∇J_X is not orthogonal to the tangent space T_eℰ for some e ∈ ℰ, hence Var_{e∈ℰ}[J_X(e)] > 0. H₃ (Dominance of thermophysiology-driven selection) states that there exists a scenario in which the TS-Engine strictly dominates an exergy-optimal hybrid MPC baseline driven by population comfort models at equal hardware, comfort, and resource constraints.

The specification includes: a full-order Fiala thermophysiological model (187 nodes, 12 anatomical elements); a reduced-order model of dimension n_r = 46 obtained via Proper Orthogonal Decomposition with a principled clothing-moisture state; a complete exergy formulation following Shukuya et al. with warm/cool and wet/dry decomposition; a mixed-integer nonlinear program with dwell-time temporal logic implemented via augmented-horizon Combinatorial Integral Approximation; a two-layer hierarchical MPC architecture with Ensemble Kalman Filter state estimation on the identifiable subspace; and a layer-by-layer validation protocol beginning with exergy-balance closure. All hypotheses are open to falsification.

