Reference state convention. The dead state is (T₀, P₀, φ₀). Two choices are defensible: (i) a constant dead state with T₀ fixed at a reference temperature (e.g., annual mean), or (ii) a time-varying reference temperature T₀(t) tracking outdoor air temperature. The Shukuya tradition uses a time-varying outdoor-air reference. This document adopts a time-varying reference temperature T₀(t) and a constant pressure P₀ and relative humidity φ₀, because a time-varying reference changes Ẋ_{D,body} = T₀(t) Ṡ_{gen,body} and therefore the objective. All exergy terms are evaluated with absolute temperatures (Kelvin). A sign-convention table is specified in Section 2.2.

2.2 Exergy: Warm/Cool and Wet/Dry Decomposition

Following Shukuya et al. (2010, 2012), exergy associated with heat and mass transfer is decomposed into orthogonal warm/cool and wet/dry contributions.

Warm/cool exergy (sensible heat transfer at absolute temperature T):

Ẋ_Q^{wc} = Q̇ (1 − T₀/T)

where Q̇ is positive for heat flow into the system and negative for heat flow out. For T < T₀ the bracket is negative, corresponding to "cool exergy." This sign duality is explicit in every term.

Wet/dry exergy of water vapor in humid air:

The chemical exergy of water vapor in air with partial pressure P_v relative to dead-state vapor pressure P_{v,0} = φ₀ P_sat(T₀) is:

Ẋ_v^{wd} = ṁ_v R_v T₀ ln(P_v / P_{v,0})

where ṁ_v is the vapor mass flow (not total air mass flow) and R_v is the specific gas constant of water vapor. The full humid-air exergy also includes dry-air displacement and mixing terms; these are treated separately in the body balance as they are not controlled by the thermal mechanisms considered here.

Liquid water exergy:

For liquid water at temperature T_s and pressure P_s, the exergy relative to liquid water at the dead state (T₀, P₀) is:

Ẋ_liq^{wd} = ṁ_liq [ (h(T_s) − h(T₀)) − T₀ (s(T_s) − s(T₀)) + v_liq (P_s − P₀) ]

where h and s are specific enthalpy and entropy from the IAPWS-95 formulation for water, and v_liq is specific volume. For near-ambient temperatures the thermal part is small; the compositional part (dispersion into vapor) dominates and is captured by the wet/dry exergy of the resulting vapor.

Radiative exergy (gray-surface approximation):

For radiative exchange between a surface at temperature T_s and surroundings at effective radiative temperature T_r, the Shukuya warm/cool formulation is:

Ẋ_rad = εσA (T_s⁴ − T_r⁴) (1 − T₀/T_r)

This is the primary definition. Spectral corrections enter through band-integrated effective temperatures and are treated as a sensitivity study, not the primary formulation. In the Shukuya tradition, T_r is the mean radiant temperature of the surrounding surfaces.

Sign convention table:

Term Positive when Negative when
Ẋ_Q^{wc} Heat into system, T > T₀ Heat out, or T < T₀
Ẋ_v^{wd} P_v > P_{v,0} P_v < P_{v,0}
Ẋ_liq^{wd} Water warmer/compressed relative to dead state Water cooler/depressurized
Ẋ_rad Net radiative absorption Net radiative emission

2.3 Human Body Exergy Balance

The human body is an open thermodynamic system. The general exergy balance following Shukuya et al. (2010) is:

dX_B/dt = Ẋ_in − Ẋ_out − Ẋ_{D,body}

with:

Ẋ_{D,body} = T₀(t) Ṡ_{gen,body} ≥ 0

Exergy inputs:

Ẋ_in = Ẋ_met + Ẋ_{air,in}^{wc} + Ẋ_{air,in}^{wd} + Ẋ_{liq,core} + Ẋ_{liq,shell} + Ẋ_{rad,in}

Exergy outputs:

Ẋ_out = Ẋ_{air,out}^{wc} + Ẋ_{air,out}^{wd} + Ẋ_{sweat} + Ẋ_{rad,out} + Ẋ_{conv,out}

Metabolic exergy. The metabolic exergy input Ẋ_met is evaluated as the chemical exergy of metabolic substrates consumed, using a conversion factor of approximately 0.95–1.0 relative to the metabolic heat rate, depending on substrate mix. The literature differs by up to a factor of 2 depending on whether metabolic heat is treated as warm exergy at body temperature or as chemical exergy of substrates. This document adopts the chemical exergy interpretation with a stated conversion factor of 0.97 (mixed substrate).

2.4 The Residual Problem and the Control-Relevant Decomposition

A critical subtlety: Ẋ_{D,body} is not directly measurable. From the balance:

Ẋ_{D,body} = Ẋ_in − Ẋ_out − dX_B/dt

Every term on the right is model-computed. This means that minimizing Ẋ_{D,body} minimizes the closure residual of the model itself. Two consequences follow.

First, Layer 0 validation (exergy-balance closure) is a hard gate before any controller results are interpretable.

Second, Ẋ_{D,body} must be decomposed into a physics-fixed part and a control-dependent part:

Ẋ_{D,body} = Ẋ_{D,met} + Ẋ_{D,transfer}

where Ẋ_{D,met} is the metabolic irreversibility (dominant, approximately 90% of total, and essentially independent of control actions) and Ẋ_{D,transfer} is the irreversible degradation at skin/clothing/environment interfaces. Only Ẋ_{D,transfer} belongs in the objective. Minimizing Ẋ_{D,met} is a constant offset that adds noise to the optimization. This decomposition is a required revision for the objective.

2.5 Hybrid System Model

The system is a switched nonlinear system:

ẋ = f_{𝐳(t)}(𝐱, 𝐮, 𝐝)

with 𝐳(t) ∈ {0,1}^{|ℳ|}. The continuous input satisfies the big-M coupling constraint:

0 ≤ u_j(t) ≤ z_j(t) u_j^max

Dwell-time constraints. For each mechanism j, the on-dwell and off-dwell constraints are:

z_j(t) − z_j(t−1) ≤ z_j(τ) ∀τ ∈ [t+1, t+τ_{j,min}^on]

z_j(t−1) − z_j(t) ≤ 1 − z_j(τ) ∀τ ∈ [t+1, t+τ_{j,min}^off]

These are temporal logic constraints. The dwell state must be carried inside the controller state, not reconstructed from history. This is implemented via an augmented-horizon formulation (Section 4.5).

Mutual exclusivity. Thermodynamic conflicts are encoded as:

A 𝐳(t) ≤ 𝐛

where A is a matrix of exclusivity constraints. For example, evaporative cooling (z_e) and dehumidification (z_d) may be mutually exclusive in certain configurations: z_e + z_d ≤ 1. The matrix A and vector 𝐛 are static (state-independent) in the primary formulation; state-dependent exclusivity is treated as a sensitivity.

