6. Propositions and Proofs

6.1 Proposition 1 (Existence and Dimension of ℰ)

Statement. Let F: ℝ^{M+P} → ℝ^q be C¹ with q = N_H + 1 + N_E. Suppose that at (𝐱_E, 𝐮) and for fixed 𝐳, rank(DF) = r. Then ℰ(𝐱_H*, 𝐳) is locally a C¹ submanifold of dimension M + P − r.

Proof. Direct application of the constant-rank theorem. The solution set F^{−1}(0) is locally a manifold of codimension r. The dimension follows. □

Remark. The rank condition is essential. Raw degree-of-freedom counting (M + P > q) is necessary but not sufficient. The rank must be verified numerically via SVD of DF for each feasible 𝐳.

Verification protocol. For each 𝐳 ∈ 𝒵_feas, compute the SVD of DF over a dense sample of ℰ(𝐱_H*, 𝐳). Report the numerical rank with tolerance. If rank(DF) < q − 1, investigate the source of rank deficiency (structural conservation law or numerical degeneracy).

6.2 Proposition 2 (Non-Constant Exergy Cost on ℰ)

Statement. Let J_X: ℰ(𝐱_H*) → ℝ be the exergy cost functional restricted to ℰ. Under non-degeneracy, there exist e₁, e₂ ∈ ℰ such that J_X(e₁) ≠ J_X(e₂).

Proof. The tangent space is T_eℰ = ker DF(e). Define the projected gradient:

g = ∇J_X − DF^T (DF DF^T)^{−1} DF ∇J_X

The projected gradient is the component of ∇J_X orthogonal to the row space of DF, i.e., the component in ker DF. H₂ is equivalent to ‖g‖ > 0 on ℰ.

Transversality argument. Consider the parameter vector θ comprising thermal properties, geometry, and dead-state conditions. The condition g(θ) = 0 for all e ∈ ℰ defines a submanifold in (e, θ)-space. By Sard's theorem on the jet space, the set of θ for which g vanishes identically on ℰ has measure zero, provided there exists at least one θ where g ≠ 0. The proof structure is: (i) transversality yields generic non-orthogonality; (ii) constructive verification yields a specific instance with ‖g‖ > 0, with SVD-based rank and a reported lower bound on ‖g‖.

Hypothesis hierarchy. H₂ presupposes H₁. If H₁ fails at some (𝐱_H, 𝐳), the variance over a point is trivially zero. The hypothesis is conditioned: H₂(𝐱_H, 𝐳) | H₁(𝐱_H*, 𝐳).

Numerical verification. Sample ℰ and compute Var_{e∈ℰ}[J_X(e)]. If variance > 0, H₂ is supported. Report variance within and between connected components. □

6.3 Proposition 3 (H₃ Dominance — Value of Information)

Statement. There exists a scenario s ∈ 𝒮 such that the Pareto front of the TS-Engine strictly dominates the Pareto front of Architecture E at equal hardware, comfort, and resource constraints:

∃s ∈ 𝒮 such that 𝒫_TS(s) ≺ 𝒫_E(s)

where ≺ denotes strict Pareto dominance of fronts:

∀p_E ∈ 𝒫_E, ∃p_TS ∈ 𝒫_TS such that p_TS dominates p_E, and not vice versa.

Baseline definition. Architecture E is an exergy-optimal hybrid MPC with the same actuator set, the same constraints, and the same objective structure as the TS-Engine, but driven by a population comfort model (PMV/SET-style) without explicit physiological state feedback. The only difference is information: E lacks 𝐱̂_H and its covariance.

Status. This is the central hypothesis. It is not proven. It is to be tested via the benchmark in Section 8.

Dominance test. Pre-register the dominance test using:

· Pareto hypervolume difference against a fixed reference point
· Additive ε-indicator
· Minimum-effect threshold (e.g., 5% hypervolume improvement)
· Paired evaluation across the 8 scenarios
· ≥10 seeds/weather realizations per scenario
· 95% confidence intervals on the hypervolume difference

A single seed or a single scenario is insufficient. Strict dominance per front is statistically meaningless without replicate variance.

6.4 Proposition 4 (Falsification Criterion for H₃)

H₃ is falsified if:

∀s ∈ 𝒮, 𝒫_TS(s) ⊀ 𝒫_E(s)

where 𝒫_E is the Pareto front of Architecture E at equal discomfort and equal resource constraints.

If F does not dominate E, the characterization of why — which mechanisms fail, in which scenarios, and by how much — is itself a scientific contribution.

