Rationale. The core time constant is 60–90 min; skin and vasomotor responses are 0.5–5 min. The slow layer captures core drift and storage scheduling, and selects mechanisms. The fast layer handles continuous modulation within the active 𝐳.

Nesting condition. The fast layer's constraint set must contain the projection of the slow layer's reachable set:

𝒞_fast(𝐱_slow) ⊇ proj_{𝐱_fast} ℛ_slow

where ℛ_slow is the slow-layer reachable set. This one-line condition prevents a whole class of closed-loop pathologies.

Fast horizon. The fast horizon is extended to 30 min (from 15 min) to be at least 3× the longest dwell time (10–20 min). This ensures that the optimizer can react to mechanism switches within the horizon.

7.3 State Estimation: Identifiable Subspace and EnKF

Observability gap. With N = 11 segments, direct measurement of all T_{s,i}, w_i, ṁ_{sw,i} is infeasible. The system is not observable from 3–4 wearable sensors alone.

Identifiability analysis. Before designing the EnKF, compute the empirical observability Gramian of the reduced-order model over the scenario ensemble. Size the EnKF state to the identifiable subspace (likely 10–20 dimensions, not 46). Estimate unidentifiable states at prior mean with prior covariance in the chance constraint. This converts the observability gap from a weakness into a designed feature.

Sensor package. The assumed wearable package is:

· Heart rate (1 Hz)
· 2–3 skin temperatures (wrist, chest, forehead; 0.1 Hz)
· Near-body air temperature and humidity (0.1 Hz)

This is consistent with the physiological-estimation literature, where heart rate and skin temperature are the highest-value wearable channels. Kalman-filter estimators of core temperature from heart rate alone achieve RMSE ≈ 0.30–0.39°C, near the ingestible-sensor accuracy, but cross-dataset generalization degrades sharply.

EnKF formulation. The posterior is:

p(𝐱_H | 𝐲) ∝ p(𝐲 | 𝐱_H) p(𝐱_H)

with prior p(𝐱_H) from the Fiala population model. Use EnKF with N_e = 100 ensemble members on the identifiable subspace. Specify localization (LETKF-style local patches per segment) and adaptive inflation to avoid spurious long-range correlations.

Uncertainty quantification. The posterior covariance Cov(𝐱̂_H) is propagated to the MPC as a chance constraint:

Pr(𝐱_H ∈ 𝒞_H) ≥ 1 − δ

The chance constraint is implemented via constraint tightening from the EnKF ensemble. The literature shows that eliminating comfort violations via constraint tightening raised energy cost by up to 7.53% in comparable studies. This trade-off must be reported explicitly as a benchmark metric.

Estimator validation layer. Before closed-loop testing, perform simulated-truth studies: use the full Fiala model as "patient" and the EnKF as estimator. Report posterior calibration (coverage of 95% credible intervals). An overconfident EnKF silently defeats the chance constraints.

