Appendix B: Proof of Proposition 2 — Non-Constant Exergy Cost on the Manifold

B.1 Statement

Let J_X: \mathcal{E}(\mathbf{x}_H^*, \mathbf{z}) \to \mathbb{R} be the exergy cost functional restricted to the thermal equivalence manifold. Under non-degeneracy, there exist e_1, e_2 \in \mathcal{E}(\mathbf{x}_H^*, \mathbf{z}) such that J_X(e_1) \neq J_X(e_2).

Equivalently, the projected gradient

g = \nabla J_X - DF^T (DF \, DF^T)^{-1} DF \, \nabla J_X

satisfies \|g\| > 0 on \mathcal{E}.

B.2 Proof via Projected Gradient

Step 1: Tangent space. The tangent space to \mathcal{E} at a point e = (\mathbf{x}_E, \mathbf{u}) is:

T_e \mathcal{E} = \ker DF(e) = \{v \in \mathbb{R}^{M+P} : DF(e) \, v = 0\}.

Step 2: Orthogonal decomposition. Decompose \mathbb{R}^{M+P} into the row space of DF(e) and its orthogonal complement:

\mathbb{R}^{M+P} = \operatorname{range}(DF^T) \oplus \ker DF.

Every vector w \in \mathbb{R}^{M+P} admits a unique decomposition w = w_{\parallel} + w_{\perp} with w_{\parallel} \in \operatorname{range}(DF^T) and w_{\perp} \in \ker DF.

Step 3: Projected gradient. The orthogonal projection of \nabla J_X onto \ker DF is:

g = \Pi_{\ker DF} \nabla J_X = \nabla J_X - DF^T (DF \, DF^T)^{-1} DF \, \nabla J_X

where \Pi_{\ker DF} = I - DF^T (DF \, DF^T)^{-1} DF is the orthogonal projection onto \ker DF. This is the standard form of the projected gradient in constrained optimization.

Step 4: Non-orthogonality implies variation. If g \neq 0, then \nabla J_X \notin \operatorname{range}(DF^T) = (T_e \mathcal{E})^\perp. Choose v = g \in T_e \mathcal{E}. Then

\langle \nabla J_X, v \rangle = \langle \nabla J_X, g \rangle = \|g\|^2 > 0.

By Taylor expansion, J_X(e + \epsilon v) = J_X(e) + \epsilon \langle \nabla J_X, v \rangle + O(\epsilon^2) \neq J_X(e) for sufficiently small \epsilon > 0. Hence J_X varies along \mathcal{E}. \square

B.3 Genericity via Transversality and Sard's Theorem

Step 1: Parameterized family. Consider the parameter vector \theta \in \Theta \subseteq \mathbb{R}^p comprising thermal properties, geometry, and dead-state conditions. The exergy functional and constraints depend on \theta:

J_X = J_X(e; \theta), \quad F = F(e; \theta).

Define the parameterized projected gradient:

g(e, \theta) = \Pi_{\ker DF(e; \theta)} \nabla_e J_X(e; \theta).

Step 2: Transversality. The condition g(e, \theta) = 0 for all e \in \mathcal{E}(\theta) defines a subset of the product manifold \mathcal{E} \times \Theta. The map

G: \mathcal{E} \times \Theta \to \mathbb{R}^{M+P}, \quad G(e, \theta) = g(e, \theta)

is a smooth map between manifolds. The zero set G^{-1}(0) is the set of parameter values for which the exergy cost is constant along \mathcal{E}.

Step 3: Sard's theorem. Sard's theorem states that the set of critical values of a smooth map has Lebesgue measure zero. Equivalently, almost all values are regular values.

Apply Sard's theorem to the projection \pi: G^{-1}(0) \to \Theta. The critical values of \pi form a set of measure zero in \Theta. For all regular values \theta \notin \pi(\text{Crit}(G)), the preimage \pi^{-1}(\theta) is a submanifold of dimension \dim G^{-1}(0) - \dim \Theta. If this dimension is negative (i.e., G^{-1}(0) is empty for generic \theta), then g(e, \theta) \neq 0 for generic \theta.

Step 4: Generic transversality. The fundamental result: transversality is a generic condition — if it fails, it can be restored by an arbitrarily small perturbation. The set of regular values of a smooth map is open and dense (by Sard's theorem and continuity), so generic non-orthogonality holds provided there exists at least one parameter value where g \neq 0.

Step 5: Constructive verification. To complete the proof, it suffices to exhibit one specific parameter instance (\theta_0) and one point e_0 \in \mathcal{E}(\theta_0) where g(e_0, \theta_0) \neq 0. This is done numerically by SVD-based rank computation and evaluation of the projected gradient at sampled points on \mathcal{E}. \square

B.4 Hypothesis Hierarchy

H₂ presupposes H₁. If H₁ fails at some (\mathbf{x}_H^*, \mathbf{z}) — i.e., \dim \mathcal{E} = 0 — then the variance over a point is trivially zero. The hypothesis is conditioned:

\text{H}_2(\mathbf{x}_H^*, \mathbf{z}) \mid \text{H}_1(\mathbf{x}_H^*, \mathbf{z}).

This hierarchy must be stated explicitly in any numerical verification.

