Appendix C: Proof of Proposition 3 — Value-of-Information Dominance

C.1 Statement

There exists a scenario s \in \mathcal{S} such that the Pareto front of the TS-Engine strictly dominates the Pareto front of Architecture E at equal hardware, comfort, and resource constraints:

\exists s \in \mathcal{S} : \mathcal{P}_F(s) \prec \mathcal{P}_E(s)

where \prec denotes strict Pareto dominance of fronts:

\forall p_E \in \mathcal{P}_E, \; \exists p_F \in \mathcal{P}_F \text{ such that } p_F \text{ dominates } p_E,

and not vice versa.

C.2 Definitions

Architecture E (baseline). An exergy-optimal hybrid MPC with the same actuator set, the same constraints, and the same objective structure as the TS-Engine, but driven by a population comfort model (PMV/SET-style) without explicit physiological state feedback. The only difference is information: E lacks \hat{\mathbf{x}}_H and its covariance.

Architecture F (TS-Engine). Same plant, same actuators, same objective structure, but with \hat{\mathbf{x}}_H and \text{Cov}(\hat{\mathbf{x}}_H) entering the constraints (chance constraints) and mechanism selection.

Architecture G (oracle). F with perfect physiological state (simulated truth).

C.3 The Value-of-Information Argument

Step 1: Information structure. Let \mathcal{I}_E denote the information available to Architecture E and \mathcal{I}_F the information available to Architecture F. The key inclusion is:

\mathcal{I}_E \subseteq \mathcal{I}_F

because F has access to all environmental information available to E plus the physiological state estimate.

Step 2: Decision space. The feasible decision sets are:

\mathcal{D}_E = \{(z, u) : \text{constraints satisfied under } \mathcal{I}_E\}

\mathcal{D}_F = \{(z, u) : \text{constraints satisfied under } \mathcal{I}_F\}

Since \mathcal{I}_E \subseteq \mathcal{I}_F, the physiological chance constraints available to F are strictly more informative. In general, \mathcal{D}_E \not\subseteq \mathcal{D}_F and \mathcal{D}_F \not\subseteq \mathcal{D}_E; the sets are incomparable without further assumptions. However, when the population comfort model used by E is conservative (as PMV typically is for individuals), F can access decisions that E must reject as infeasible.

Step 3: Value of information. The expected value of information (EVPI) in decision theory is the maximum amount a decision-maker would pay for perfect information. The classical result is that EVPI \ge 0 for expected-utility maximizers, and non-negativity holds under a weak dominance axiom even for non-expected-utility preferences.

For multi-criterion problems under uncertainty, the value of additional information is defined analogously: it is the improvement in the Pareto front achievable by expanding the information set.

Step 4: Dominance condition. Architecture F strictly dominates E in scenario s if:

\forall p_E \in \mathcal{P}_E(s), \; \exists p_F \in \mathcal{P}_F(s) : p_F \text{ dominates } p_E

and there exists p_F \in \mathcal{P}_F(s) that is not dominated by any element of \mathcal{P}_E(s).

This is a partial order on Pareto fronts, not a total order. The claim is existential: there exists at least one scenario where the dominance holds.

Step 5: Falsification. H₃ is falsified if:

\forall s \in \mathcal{S}, \; \mathcal{P}_F(s) \not\prec \mathcal{P}_E(s)

i.e., if for every scenario, there exists at least one point on the E-front that is not dominated by any point on the F-front. \square

C.4 Statistical Testing Protocol

Pareto dominance of fronts is tested using:

1. Hypervolume indicator. For a front \mathcal{P} with reference point r, the hypervolume \mathcal{H}(\mathcal{P}, r) is the volume of the region dominated by \mathcal{P} and bounded above by r. F dominates E in hypervolume if \mathcal{H}(\mathcal{P}_F, r) > \mathcal{H}(\mathcal{P}_E, r).
2. Additive \varepsilon-indicator. The minimum \varepsilon such that for every p_E \in \mathcal{P}_E, there exists p_F \in \mathcal{P}_F with p_F + \varepsilon \mathbf{1} \succeq p_E.
3. Replicate protocol. Run \ge 10 seeds/weather realizations per scenario. Compute 95% confidence intervals on the hypervolume difference. Require a minimum-effect threshold (e.g., 5% hypervolume improvement) for a dominance claim.

