Appendix D: Reduced-Order Model Construction and Error Analysis

D.1 POD Basis Construction

Let \{\mathbf{x}_H^{\text{full}}(t_s)\}_{s=1}^{N_s} \subset \mathbb{R}^{n_H^{\text{full}}} be a set of snapshot solutions of the full-order Fiala model, with n_H^{\text{full}} = 187. Form the snapshot matrix:

S = [\mathbf{x}_H^{\text{full}}(t_1), \ldots, \mathbf{x}_H^{\text{full}}(t_{N_s})] \in \mathbb{R}^{n_H^{\text{full}} \times N_s}.

Compute the SVD:

S = U \Sigma V^T

where U \in \mathbb{R}^{n_H^{\text{full}} \times N_s}, \Sigma = \text{diag}(\sigma_1, \ldots, \sigma_{N_s}) with \sigma_1 \ge \sigma_2 \ge \cdots \ge \sigma_{N_s} \ge 0, and V \in \mathbb{R}^{N_s \times N_s}.

The POD basis of dimension n_r is the first n_r left singular vectors:

\Phi = [u_1, \ldots, u_{n_r}] \in \mathbb{R}^{n_H^{\text{full}} \times n_r}.

The reduced-order approximation is:

\mathbf{x}_H^{\text{full}}(t) \approx \bar{\mathbf{x}}_H + \Phi \tilde{\mathbf{x}}_H(t)

where \bar{\mathbf{x}}_H is the time-averaged field.

D.2 A Priori POD Error Bound

Theorem (POD projection error). Let \{\phi_j\}_{j=1}^{r} be the POD basis functions corresponding to the r largest singular values \{\sigma_j\} obtained from the SVD of the snapshot matrix. Then, for any snapshot \psi(t_n), the projection error satisfies:

\left\| \psi(t_n) - \sum_{j=1}^{r} \langle \psi(t_n), \phi_j \rangle \phi_j \right\|_{L^2(\Omega)}^2 \le \sum_{j=r+1}^{N} \sigma_j^2.

Proof sketch. The truncated SVD provides the optimal rank-r approximation in the Frobenius norm. The error is the sum of the squares of the neglected singular values. \square

D.3 DEIM Hyper-Reduction for Nonlinear Terms

Plain POD does not reduce the computational cost of evaluating the nonlinear right-hand side f_H(\mathbf{x}_H, \mathbf{x}_E, \mathbf{z}, \mathbf{u}), which involves the perfusion and sweat terms. The Discrete Empirical Interpolation Method (DEIM) addresses this.

DEIM approximation. Given a nonlinear function f: \mathbb{R}^N \to \mathbb{R}^N, construct a POD basis U \in \mathbb{R}^{N \times m} from snapshots of f. The DEIM approximation is:

f \approx \hat{f} = U (P^T U)^{-1} P^T f

where P \in \mathbb{R}^{N \times m} is a selection matrix determined by the DEIM algorithm (greedy selection of interpolation indices via QR with column pivoting).

DEIM error bound. The approximation error satisfies:

\|f - \hat{f}\|_2 \le \|(P^T U)^{-1}\|_2 \|(I - U U^T) f\|_2.

The quantity \|(I - U U^T) f\|_2 is the POD projection error, which decreases as more basis vectors are included. The quantity \|(P^T U)^{-1}\|_2 is kept bounded by the DEIM index selection algorithm. A sharper bound is achievable via strong rank-revealing QR factorization.

D.4 Practical Error Verification Protocol

1. Snapshot ensemble. Generate snapshots spanning all 8 benchmark scenarios × all feasible mechanism combinations (or a designed subset via Latin hypercube sampling).
2. Modal energy criterion. Choose n_r such that:

\frac{\sum_{j=1}^{n_r} \sigma_j^2}{\sum_{j=1}^{N_s} \sigma_j^2} \ge 0.999.

3. A posteriori error estimation. On validation trajectories not in the snapshot set, compute the projection error:

\varepsilon_{\text{red}}(t) = \|\mathbf{x}_H^{\text{full}}(t) - \Pi(\tilde{\mathbf{x}}_H(t))\|.

Report max and RMS of \varepsilon_{\text{red}} over the validation set.

4. Hyper-reduction error. Compute the DEIM error bound on validation trajectories and verify it is dominated by the POD error.

