Appendix E: Ensemble Kalman Filter with Localization and Inflation

E.1 Formulation

The EnKF approximates the Bayesian posterior p(\mathbf{x}_H | \mathbf{y}) using an ensemble of N_e state vectors \{\mathbf{x}_H^{(i)}\}_{i=1}^{N_e}.

Forecast step. Propagate each ensemble member through the ROM:

\mathbf{x}_H^{(i),f}(t+1) = \mathcal{M}(\mathbf{x}_H^{(i),a}(t), \mathbf{u}(t), \mathbf{z}(t)) + \boldsymbol{\eta}^{(i)}(t)

where \boldsymbol{\eta}^{(i)} \sim \mathcal{N}(0, Q) is process noise.

Analysis step. Update each member using the Kalman gain:

\mathbf{x}_H^{(i),a} = \mathbf{x}_H^{(i),f} + K (\mathbf{y} + \boldsymbol{\epsilon}^{(i)} - \mathcal{H}(\mathbf{x}_H^{(i),f}))

where \mathcal{H} is the observation operator, \boldsymbol{\epsilon}^{(i)} \sim \mathcal{N}(0, R), and

K = P^f H^T (H P^f H^T + R)^{-1}.

The sample covariance is:

P^f = \frac{1}{N_e - 1} \sum_{i=1}^{N_e} (\mathbf{x}_H^{(i),f} - \bar{\mathbf{x}}_H^f)(\mathbf{x}_H^{(i),f} - \bar{\mathbf{x}}_H^f)^T.

E.2 Localization

With finite ensemble size, the sample covariance P^f has rank at most N_e - 1 and contains spurious long-range correlations. Localization replaces P^f with its Schur product with a localization matrix \rho:

P^f_{\text{loc}} = \rho \circ P^f

where \rho is a compactly supported correlation function (e.g., Gaspari–Cohn) that decays with distance. Shrinkage localization and multiplicative inflation are effective in high-dimensional data assimilation. The Schur product approach ensures that observations at a particular point influence the analysis only within a local radius.

E.3 Inflation

To counteract ensemble collapse, multiply the forecast covariance by an inflation factor 1 + \delta:

P^f_{\text{infl}} = (1 + \delta) P^f_{\text{loc}}.

Additive inflation adds a small multiple of the identity matrix to the background covariance. Prior inflation is inversely proportional to ensemble size:

\text{inflation factor} = 1 + \frac{1}{N_e}.

E.4 Identifiable Subspace

Before constructing the EnKF, compute the empirical observability Gramian of the ROM over the scenario ensemble:

\mathcal{W}_o = \sum_{t=0}^{T} \Phi(t, 0)^T \mathcal{H}^T \mathcal{H} \Phi(t, 0)

where \Phi(t, 0) is the state transition matrix. Compute the eigendecomposition of \mathcal{W}_o. The identifiable subspace is spanned by eigenvectors with eigenvalues above a threshold (e.g., 10^{-6} relative to the largest). Size the EnKF state to the identifiable subspace (likely 10–20 dimensions, not 46). Estimate unidentifiable states at prior mean with prior covariance in the chance constraint.

E.5 Chance Constraint Implementation

The chance constraint \Pr(\mathbf{x}_H \in \mathcal{C}_H) \ge 1 - \delta is implemented via constraint tightening. For Gaussian-distributed state estimates, the tightened constraint is:

\bar{\mathbf{x}}_H \in \mathcal{C}_H \ominus \kappa(\delta) \sqrt{\text{diag}(P^a)}

where \kappa(\delta) = \Phi^{-1}(1-\delta) is the standard normal quantile and \ominus denotes Minkowski erosion. The literature shows that eliminating comfort violations via constraint tightening raised energy cost by up to 7.53% in comparable studies; this trade-off must be reported explicitly.

