Appendix F: Combinatorial Integral Approximation with Dwell-Time Constraints

F.1 CIA Decomposition

The CIA decomposition solves the MIOCP in three steps:

1. Relaxation. Relax the binary controls \mathbf{z} \in \{0,1\}^{|\mathcal{M}|} to continuous controls \mathbf{z} \in [0,1]^{|\mathcal{M}|}. Solve the resulting NLP using direct multiple shooting (e.g., with CasADi).
2. Rounding. Solve the CIA problem: given the relaxed solution \mathbf{z}^*(t), find a binary control \mathbf{z}(t) \in \{0,1\}^{|\mathcal{M}|} that minimizes the integral deviation:

\min_{\mathbf{z} \in \{0,1\}^{|\mathcal{M}|}} \left\| \int_0^T (\mathbf{z}^*(t) - \mathbf{z}(t)) \, dt \right\|_\infty

subject to dwell-time and mutual-exclusivity constraints.

3. Simulation. Simulate the system with the rounded binary controls.

F.2 Dwell-Time Constraints in CIA

Minimum dwell time constraints are incorporated into the CIA problem as combinatorial constraints. For a minimum on-dwell time \tau_{\min}^{\text{on}} and off-dwell time \tau_{\min}^{\text{off}}:

z_j(t) = 1 \implies z_j(\tau) = 1 \quad \forall \tau \in [t, t + \tau_{\min}^{\text{on}}]

z_j(t) = 0 \implies z_j(\tau) = 0 \quad \forall \tau \in [t, t + \tau_{\min}^{\text{off}}]

These constraints are implemented in the MILP formulation of the CIA problem. The pycombina tool efficiently formulates CIA problems, includes combinatorial constraints, and solves the resulting MILP using a tailored branch-and-bound solver.

F.3 Augmented-Horizon Formulation

The augmented-horizon concept from Jansen et al. (2024) extends the combinatorial horizon backwards to the dwell trigger, so that the CIA problem sees the full dwell constraint from the current state. This ensures that the dwell constraint is satisfied from the current time onward, not just within the prediction horizon.

Implementation. Let t_{\text{trigger}} be the time of the most recent switch for mechanism j. The augmented horizon is [t_{\text{trigger}}, T]. The CIA problem is solved over this extended horizon with the dwell constraint applied from t_{\text{trigger}}.

F.4 Dynamic Programming-Inspired CIA

A recent dynamic programming-inspired approach solves the CIA problem with dwell-time constraints more efficiently than general-purpose MILP solvers. The method uses a flexible cost-to-go function that adapts to various value functions, making it suitable for cases where simple approximations of the relaxed solution are insufficient.

F.5 Move-Blocking

Move-blocking is not applied to binary variables, as it conflicts with dwell constraints and CIA rounding guarantees. For continuous inputs, move-blocking is optional: fix \mathbf{u} for the first N_{\text{block}} steps and relax to continuous for later steps.

